---
import Document from "@/layouts/Document.astro";
import { currentRevision } from "@/lib/atlas";
import { getDataset } from "@/lib/data";
import { passiveChecks } from "@/lib/scanner";

const data = getDataset();
const revision = currentRevision(data);
const scannerChecks = passiveChecks(data).length;
const repo = "https://github.com/letsauthlab/letsauth";
const issue = `${repo}/issues/new?template=observation.yml`;
const entry = `- server: stripe                     # must equal the file's server id
  feature: mcp.as.cimd               # a feature with party mcp-server or authorization-server
  revision: { spec: mcp-authorization, revision: "${revision.id}" }
  status: supported                  # supported | partial | unsupported | unknown | not-applicable
  method: passive-scan               # manual | vendor-claim | passive-scan | active-scan
  observedAt: 2026-10-13T18:40:00Z
  observedBy: jbspeakr               # your handle, or scanner@<version>
  evidence: https://auth.stripe.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server`;
---

<Document
  title="Contribute"
  heading="Add what you know, with evidence"
  description="How to add an observation, correct a cell, list a server or withdraw one from the MCP auth atlas. Two paths, both reviewed before anything is published."
  path="/atlas/mcp/contribute/"
  eyebrow="Contribute"
  crumbs={[{ href: "/atlas/mcp/", label: "MCP auth atlas" }]}
  lede="The atlas is a dataset in a git repository. Anyone can add an observation or correct one; a maintainer reviews the evidence before it is published. Nothing is ever edited in place."
>
  <h2>Two ways in</h2>
  <p>
    Pick the one that fits what you have. Both end in a reviewed pull request, and the result
    looks the same in the atlas.
  </p>
  <ul class="cards" role="list">
    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Path A</p>
      <h3>Open an issue</h3>
      <p>
        The quickest route. The form asks for the same fields as a data entry: server, feature,
        revision, status, method, date, evidence and your handle. A maintainer turns an accepted
        issue into a pull request and links the issue as evidence where the issue itself is the
        evidence.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button primary" href={issue} rel="noopener">Open the issue form</a></p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Path B</p>
      <h3>Open a pull request</h3>
      <p>
        Append an entry to the server's observation file yourself. Validation runs locally and
        again in CI, so a reviewer only has to open the evidence. The steps are below.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button" href={repo} rel="noopener">Open the repository</a></p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The pull request, step by step</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      Append an entry to <code>data/observations/&lt;server-id&gt;.yaml</code>. Create the file if
      the server has none.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run <code>pnpm validate:data</code>. Failures name the file, the entry index and the field,
      for example <code>data/observations/stripe.yaml[3].evidence: required (URL or note)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Open the pull request. CI repeats the validation and the append-only check.</li>
    <li>A maintainer reviews the evidence and merges. The next deploy publishes it.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Entry format</h3>
  <pre><code>{entry}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>Evidence</strong> is a URL a reviewer can open (a metadata document, a
    documentation page, a response capture) or a precise note. <strong>Method</strong> says
    how you know: <code>manual</code> for something you checked by hand,
    <code>vendor-claim</code> for what the vendor says, <code>passive-scan</code> for reading
    public metadata, <code>active-scan</code> for sending requests. When two observations of a
    cell exist, the newest one is shown; equal timestamps are broken by method strength
    (active-scan over passive-scan over manual over vendor-claim).
  </p>

  <h2>Let the scanner write it</h2>
  <p>
    For the {scannerChecks} requirements the <a href="/scanner/">passive scanner</a> can read
    from public metadata, prefer <code>pnpm scan &lt;server-id&gt; --write</code> over a hand-written
    entry. It appends exactly the observations whose status or evidence changed, with evidence
    anyone can reproduce by running the same command. The nightly run does the same for every
    listed server and opens its own pull request.
  </p>

  <h2>The append-only rule</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__title">Observations are never edited or removed</p>
    <p>
      A correction is a new entry with a newer <code>observedAt</code>; the old one stays in the
      history on the server page. CI fails any pull request that changes or deletes an existing
      entry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Listing a server</h2>
  <p>
    Add <code>data/servers/&lt;id&gt;.yaml</code> with the endpoint, transport and
    <code>listed</code>. If you maintain the server, use <code>listed: opt-in</code>; otherwise
    give the <code>source</code> that lists it publicly. Servers are listed as they appear in
    public directories; nobody is scored or ranked.
  </p>

  <h2>Withdrawing a server</h2>
  <p>
    If you maintain a server and do not want it listed, use the issue form with
    "withdraw my server" and a way to verify that you maintain it. A pull request then sets
    <code>withdrawn</code> on the server. It leaves the matrix and the export; its observations
    stay in the repository history.
  </p>

  <h2>Licences</h2>
  <p>
    Data you contribute is published under <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE-DATA`} rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>;
    the site's code is <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`} rel="noopener">MIT</a>.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Quick links</p>
      <ul class="links" role="list">
        <li><a href={issue} rel="noopener">Issue form</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${repo}/tree/main/data/observations`} rel="noopener">Observation files</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/atlas-maintenance.md`} rel="noopener">Maintenance guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/atlas/mcp/data.json">The atlas as JSON</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</Document>

<style>
  .links {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .links a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .links a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
